# Migrating `EventProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Problems in the Original Code

| Issue | Why it breaks on .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — removed in .NET 5+ |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Works but is [obsolete](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.threading.thread.interrupt) and unreliable for `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` |
| Manual `Queue` + lock + `ManualResetEvent` | Reimplements what `Channel<T>` or `BlockingCollection<T>` already provide |
| Race condition | Multiple rapid `Enqueue` calls can signal once but only dequeue once — items get stuck or `InvalidOperationException` on empty dequeue |
| No cancellation | No cooperative cancellation pattern (`CancellationToken`) |
| No graceful drain | `Stop()` discards remaining queued items |

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>(
        new UnboundedChannelOptions { SingleReader = true });
    private Task? _processingTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — drain remaining items if desired
            while (_channel.Reader.TryRead(out var remaining))
            {
                Handle(remaining);
            }
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel is closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _processingTask is null) return;

        _channel.Writer.Complete();  // signal no more items

        // Wait for drain, then cancel if it takes too long
        using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
        try
        {
            await _processingTask.WaitAsync(timeoutCts.Token);
        }
        catch (TimeoutException)
        {
            await _cts.CancelAsync(); // cooperative cancellation replaces Abort()
            await _processingTask;     // wait for loop to acknowledge
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // Your processing logic here
    }
}
```

---

## Migration Pitfalls to Watch For

**1. `Thread.Abort()` is gone — no replacement.**
There is no way to forcibly kill a thread in modern .NET. You *must* use cooperative cancellation via `CancellationToken`. If `Handle()` does long-running work, pass the token into it and check `ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()`.

**2. The original code has a race condition.**
Two fast `Enqueue` calls → one `Set()` is seen → one `Dequeue()` runs → second item sits orphaned. `Channel<T>` eliminates this entirely because `ReadAllAsync` pulls *all* available items.

**3. `Thread.Interrupt()` only works on managed waits.**
It throws `ThreadInterruptedException` inside `Thread.Sleep`, `Monitor.Wait`, etc., but **not** reliably inside `WaitOne()` on all platforms. The original `Stop()` was already fragile.

**4. Sync → Async caller changes.**
Callers that called `Stop()` synchronously now need `await StopAsync()`. If you're stuck in a sync context:
```csharp
processor.StopAsync().AsTask().GetAwaiter().GetResult(); // last resort
```

**5. Bounded vs. Unbounded channels.**
The original `Queue` was unbounded (OOM risk). If you want backpressure:
```csharp
Channel.CreateBounded<string>(new BoundedChannelOptions(1000)
{
    FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait
});
// Then use: await _channel.Writer.WriteAsync(item);
```

**6. Exception handling.**
If `Handle()` throws in the original code, the thread dies silently. Add error handling inside the loop or let the exception propagate to `_processingTask` where `StopAsync` will surface it.
